  "agentNotes": [],
  "description": "Four agents reading four different Artifacts independently derived the same finding: there is no separate venue allowlist. A zero rate limit makes every call revert, so configuring a limit IS granting",
  "orientation": "Four agents reading four different Artifacts independently derived the same finding: there is no separate venue allowlist. A zero rate limit makes every call revert, so configuring a limit IS granting access, and sizing access IS the same governance act. This eliminates the classic whitelisted-but-unlimited bug class and gives a per-counterparty kill switch for free. The costs are equally structural: a zeroed venue is indistinguishable on-chain from one never approved, and de-listing a counterparty while an async redemption is pending makes the claim revert, stranding committed funds. Grove's Curve handling shows the mature form \u2014 an imbalanced deposit is charged against the SWAP limit too, because limits must key on economic effect rather than function name.",
  "openQuestions": [],
  "links": []
